QuickJS-ng v0.17.0 (https://github.com/quickjs-ng/quickjs, tag v0.17.0): the engine only
(no quickjs-libc, no tools). MIT: LICENSE. NetSurf's JavaScript engine on Onyx
(third_party/netsurf/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/).

Onyx patches (marked Onyx):
- cutils.h: __ONYX__ has no threads (JS_HAVE_THREADS 0), its clock is gettimeofday.
- quickjs.c: __ONYX__ has no Atomics (CONFIG_ATOMICS off).
- quickjs.c: __ONYX__'s clock (gettimeofday) is the local time: js_onyx_utc_offset_min (set by
  the embedder: NetSurf's qjs_intl.h, from SD:/etc/system.ini's timezone=) is taken off Date.now
  and is getTimezoneOffset's answer, so Date's time values are UTC.
- quickjs.c, the interpreter's loop (JS_CallInternal), measured wins (callgrind instruction
  counts, tools/tests/netsurf/jit/bench.sh; React server rendering -11.5 %, Octane -1..-6 %):
  OP_strict_eq / OP_strict_neq decide ints, same-object / symbol pointers, strings of different
  lengths or both unique atoms, and undefined / null inline (OP_STRICT_EQ, js_strict_eq_slow
  otherwise); JS_FreeValue's decrement is inlined in the loop (js_free_value_inl); a string
  atom is pushed as its own value (OP_push_atom_value, OP_typeof) without JS_AtomToValue;
  js_poll_interrupts is always inlined. for...of over a dense array reads its elements
  directly (js_array_iterator_next).
- quickjs.c, quickjs.h: JS_GetBuildId (the version and quickjs.c's build date: NetSurf's code
  cache keeps bytecode for the build that wrote it).
- quickjs.c: map_hash_key mixes an object / symbol key's pointer (murmur3's finalizer): the
  upstream pointer * 3163 left the bucket index's low bits zero (aligned pointers), one bucket
  in 8-16 used -- Map / Set / WeakMap lookups by object (Vue's reactivity) walked long chains
  (docs/06-NETSURF-CHANGES.md §28).
- quickjs-jit.c (new), quickjs.c, quickjs.h: a baseline JIT for AArch64 (CONFIG_QJS_JIT, on
  __aarch64__; off until JS_SetJIT(rt, threshold, code_alloc)): JSFunctionBytecode.jit_code /
  jit_count, JSRuntime.jit_threshold / jit_steps; JS_CallInternal runs a function's machine
  code after its frame is set up, and runs one instruction for it on request
  (JS_CALL_FLAG_JIT_STEP: the frame resumed from a copy of the instruction followed by
  OP_JIT_STEP_END, an invalid opcode whose dispatch returns); backtraces map a pc in such a
  copy back (js_frame_pc); the code is freed with its function. CONFIG_QJS_NO_JIT leaves it
  out. NetSurf's docs/06 section 30 describes it.

Built into libquickjs.a by user/netsurf/Makefile (-std=gnu11 -D__ONYX__; committed like the other
libraries); the PC bench (tools/tests/netsurf/host.mk) compiles the same four files.
